    "filename": "Paraná River Floodplain, Northern Argentina.jpg",
    "title": "File:Paraná River Floodplain, Northern Argentina.jpg",
    "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/Paran%C3%A1_River_Floodplain%2C_Northern_Argentina.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
    "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paran%C3%A1_River_Floodplain,_Northern_Argentina.jpg",
    "width": 4288,
    "height": 2850,
    "mime": "image/jpeg",
    "license": "Public domain",
    "artist": "ISS Expedition 27 crew",
    "credit": "NASA Earth Observatory",
    "source": "Paraná River Floodplain, Northern Argentina",
    "description": "This astronaut photograph shows a 29-kilometre stretch of the Paraná, downstream of the small city of Goya, Argentina (just off the top left of the image).\nThe Paraná River ranges up to 3 kilometres wide along the reach illustrated in this image. The main channel is deep enough to allow smaller ocean-going ships to pass north to the capital city of Asunción, Paraguay, fully 1,200 kilometres inland and well out of the image.\nThe river’s dark brown shading indicates a heavy load of muddy sediment; smaller side channels also carry this mud. Numerous lakes are typical on active floodplains, and appear here as irregular bodies of water. Some appear brown, indicating that they probably have been refilled during recent rises and floods of the active channels.\nThe Paraná floodplain occupies the entire image; it is so wide—18 kilometres in this view—that its banks are not visible. Numerous curved, meandering channels are the most prominent characteristic of the floodplain, indicating prior positions of the river and its channels. As riverbeds move laterally by natural processes, they leave remnants of their channels, which appear as lakes and finally fill with mud. This is an excellent image for illustrating these meander forms.\n\nFrom a geological standpoint, it is interesting that almost all of the old channels are similar in curvature to today’s side channels. However, almost none of them seem to show prior positions of the main, wide Paraná channel.",
    "dateCreated": "2011-04-09",
